WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.500
Small GovCon teams do not need another blank page.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.232
DocDraft turns solicitation requirements and approved reusable language
into a structured first draft.

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:14.800
For this walkthrough, we use NASA’s public SEWP VI IT solicitation

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.276
and Aster Vale Technology, a fictional small contractor
built entirely from sanitized demo content.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.000
First, proposal staff load company-approved answers for service delivery,
supply-chain risk, quality management, and transition.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.800
Then they bring in the solicitation requirements.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:34.568
The response set stays organized requirement by requirement,
with the source language and reusable context in view.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.800
From that starting point, DocDraft prepares a response for each requirement
using the company context and relevant library entries.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:53.888
Instead of drafting from scratch, the team gets a structured working set:
four requirements, four first drafts—ready for proposal review, not final submission.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.200
The proposal lead remains in control.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:05.500
They edit language, save the revision, redraft a weak section,
or mark an answer unsure when it needs another look.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.736
The team decides what is accurate, responsive, and ready to use.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.300
When the response set is ready for the next stage,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.336
one click exports the reviewed questions and answers to a Word document.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:26.900
Founding pilots are open for small GovCon teams
using public or sanitized material.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.000
Bring your next proposal into focus at info@docdraft.ai.
